    <heading level="1">3.3. Self-Check Exercises</heading>
    <exercise number="1" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the derivative of</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f(x) = - \frac{1}{\sqrt{2x^{2} - 1}}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Rewriting, we have</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$f(x) = - {(2x^{2} - 1)}^{- 1 \slash 2}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Using the General Power Rule, we find</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {f^{\prime}(x) = - \frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}{(2x^{2} - 1)}^{- 1 \slash 2}} \\ {\phantom{f^{\prime}(x)} = - \left( - \frac{1}{2} \right){(2x^{2} - 1)}^{- 3 \slash 2}\frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}(2x^{2} - 1)} \\ {\phantom{f^{\prime}(x)} = \frac{1}{2}{(2x^{2} - 1)}^{- 3 \slash 2}(4x)} \\ {\phantom{f^{\prime}(x)} = \frac{2x}{{(2x^{2} - 1)}^{3 \slash 2}}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Female Life Expectancy Suppose the life expectancy at birth (in years) of a female in a certain country is described by the function</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$g(t) = 50.02{(1 + 1.09t)}^{0.1} (0 \leq t \leq 150)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>where $$t$$ is measured in years, with $$t = 0$$ corresponding to the beginning of 1900.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>What is the life expectancy at birth of a female born at the beginning of 1980? At the beginning of 2010?</item>
          <item>How fast is the life expectancy at birth of a female born at any time $$t$$ changing?</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>The life expectancy at birth of a female born at the beginning of 1980 is given by</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$g(80) = 50.02\left\lbrack 1 + 1.09(80) \right\rbrack^{0.1} \approx 78.29$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>or approximately $$78$$ years. Similarly, the life expectancy at birth of a female born at the beginning of the year 2010 is given by</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$g(110) = 50.02\left\lbrack 1 + 1.09(110) \right\rbrack^{0.1} \approx 80.80$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>or approximately $$81$$ years.</paragraph>
        <paragraph>The rate of change of the life expectancy at birth of a female born at any time $$t$$ is given by $$g^{\prime}(t)$$ . Using the General Power Rule, we have</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {g^{\prime}(t) = 50.02\frac{d}{\mathit{dt}}{(1 + 1.09t)}^{0.1}} \\ {\phantom{g^{\prime}(t)} = {(50.02)}{(0.1)}{(1 + 1.09t)}^{- 0.9}\frac{d}{\mathit{dt}}(1 + 1.09t)} \\ {\phantom{g^{\prime}(t)} = {(50.02)}{(0.1)}(1.09){(1 + 1.09t)}^{- 0.9}} \\ {\phantom{g^{\prime}(t)} = 5.45218{(1 + 1.09t)}^{- 0.9}} \\ {\phantom{g^{\prime}(t)} = \frac{5.45218}{{(1 + 1.09t)}^{0.9}}} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
