    <exercise number="1" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>In Section 3.1, we proved that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}(x^{n}) = nx^{n - 1}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>for the special case when $$n = 2$$ . Use the Chain Rule to show that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}(x^{1 \slash n}) = \frac{1}{n}x^{1 \slash n - 1}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>for any nonzero integer $$n$$ , assuming that $$f(x) = x^{1 \slash n}$$ is differentiable.</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Hint: Let $$f(x) = x^{1 \slash n}$$ , so that $$\left\lbrack f(x) \right\rbrack^{n} = x$$ . Differentiate both sides with respect to $$x$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="2" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>With the aid of Exercise 89, prove that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}(x^{r}) = rx^{r - 1}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>for every rational number $$r$$ .</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Hint: Let $$r = m \slash n$$ , where $$m$$ and $$n$$ are integers, with $$n \neq 0$$ , and write $$x^{r} = \left( x^{m} \right)^{1 \slash n}$$ .</paragraph>
